winecoreaudio: playback follows the Mac's output device

A stream's audio unit is tied to one device for good. A game started on the Mac's speakers
kept playing through them after headphones were connected (Bluetooth ones in particular,
which become the Mac's output when they connect), although every Mac app moves over, and a
Windows program that opened "the default device" expects the same.

Playback streams opened on the device that was the Mac's output at the time are now kept in a
list, and Core Audio is asked to say when the output device changes. Each of those streams then
gets a new audio unit on the new device, set up the way the first was: the mixer between the
stream and the device is made again as well, because the new device can have another channel
layout. The old unit is stopped before the stream is pointed at the new one and the new one is
started after, so the callback never sees half of each. The stream's buffer, position and
format are untouched; the program notices nothing but a gap of a few hundredths of a second.

The work is done on a queue of its own, not in Core Audio's notification, and is tried again
for about three seconds: a Bluetooth device is announced as the output a moment before it can
be opened. A stream opened on a device that was not the output (one picked by name in a
game's settings) stays where it is, as do capture streams. FINEWINE_AUDIO_FOLLOW_OUTPUT=0
turns it off. FINEWINE_AUDIO_REOPEN_AFTER=<seconds> re-opens the streams' device once, for
tests/runtime/audio-follow.c, which checks that playback carries on across the swap.

